          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between E11 and E11.9, and when is each the right thing to quote?",
          "answer": "The difference between E11 and E11.9 relates to the specificity of the coding for type 2 diabetes. \n\n- **E11** is a general code for type 2 diabetes. It is part of a family of codes that include different complications and characteristics of the condition. Thus, E11 can be followed by additional characters that detail specific complications such as kidney issues or neuropathy. According to the coding guidance, this code is never used without further specification (i.e., it needs additional characters) (source: coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\n- **E11.9**, on the other hand, is used when diabetes is documented without any complications. It serves as a more specific code that indicates the presence of type 2 diabetes but clarifies that there are no complications associated with it (source: coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nIn summary, use E11 for type 2 diabetes with complications and E11.9 when there are none.\n\nI could not cover specific situations or examples of when each code might be appropriately used in practice. For detailed coding guidance or clinical scenarios, consulting a coding specialist or clinician is advisable.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
